ishness to them that you you  kind of block everything else out and  the only thing that matters is  being the best and winning that  championship so I can relate somewhat  because it's very easy to get in that  trap and kind of lose the big picture if  you will in life and get so determined  on just this this one aspect and it's a  very I want to say it's a selfish thing  but it's also the trait that you have to  have to be successful have you watched  the Jordan documentary I have the I  think it's terrific you played golf with  him  I played golf with him a long time ago  and I'm actually in the process of  joining his club there in Florida the  Grove that you know which is really a  cool place he did an exceptional job  there  how do you how do you apply like well  how does that work where you you call  Michael and say I'm thinking about being  a member or you get invited you have you  have to be invited and so fortunately I  know a number of members there and I'm  going to be my wife and I just bought a  lot fairly close there it's only about  10-15 minutes away and so it would be a  great place to play in practice how good  was Jordan back when you played he's  been around a for I think I don't know  if he's still a four handicap so I  haven't played with them in a while but  I've seen him play and he seems to get  better and better and the this area that  he gets better on are the shots around  the Greens all the subtleties the  nuances putting all the intricacies the  the swing is I'm not really I haven't  seen him hit it enough to see how much  better he is but the touch around the  greens he continues to get better at who  came up with the idea to have you with  tiger and Peyton and Tom Brady that's a  good question I don't know where it  really came from there's a gentleman  Brian Zarif who does the ray Donovan  show on Showtime he's kind of our  executive producer he and I become  friends and we've been talking about the  the match for for quite a long time in  fact you know were the ones that kind of  came up with the idea years ago and  brought it to CAA and had it developed  before we've brought it to Tiger and  he's very he's got a very creative mind  and thought that the interaction between  two other players and as a partner was  going to be an important part of  improving our match because when tiger  and I came down the stretch in Vegas we  claimed  duh we didn't talk we didn't communicate  we tightened up and we needed a partner  there to to get something out of us and  so because we won't have caddies Brady  and I will be talking about each shot  club selection windbreak and so forth  it's almost like I'll be his caddy he'll  be mine and we'll be feeding off of each  other and so you'll hear a lot more  interaction plus the fact that Brady and  Manning especially is such a good smack  talker it will be entertaining because  of that you know he is so funny in the  way he delivers his lines Manning is  that you he elicits a response of  laughter rather than a defensive  response which a lot of times when  you're when you're cutting somebody up  but you can come in a little a little  hot and you can get defensive and Manny  does such a good job of making it funny  is he the best smack talker out of all  four I think so because it's so subtle  and understated and he just has a  delivery that's soft but the jabs sting  well he's got that aw shucks and he sort  of talks out the side of his mouth he's  got that Southern drawl just a little  bit just let it you know slip out a  little bit that's exactly so he he will  get the best out of tiger and I think  that he'll be the kind of the one to  deliver the smack talk and Tiger will be  the one to deliver the great shots which  is fine and then Brady and I you know  Tom and I we usually have some pretty  good responses too so I think it's going  to be a much more entertaining match  than the first one because there'll be a  lot better interactive and you've played  with Brady Peyton's played with Tiger  and you played with Tom is that how the  team's formed I think so I've played a  number of rounds with Tom now and in  fact I had one of the greatest moments  Dan of my one of those interesting  moments that occurred when I did play  with Tom it was about a week or two  before he was going to start throwing  with Edelman getting ready for training  camp and we were at Augusta National and  he asked if I would go catch some passes  I said yeah man I loved it loved it fast  Tom Brady were twenty yards away it's  7:15 in the morning and it's half dark  out and he's thrown  these balls so hard that I'm worrying  about busting a finger  I only catch the half the second half of  the pass and so if the ball wasn't on my  torso I couldn't catch it it would if it  was off to the side I would end up  missing it and I was so cautious about  bending my fingers in a way you know  having them so that I don't Jam a finger  if there were three times that a ball  hit the palm that it got through my  fingers and hit the palm I handed all  three times I had a nerve shot go right  up my arm and I thought that's gotta be  so hard to be running full-speed with a  helmet on bobbing up and down and have  these balls coming in with such heat and  grab him it gave me a new respect for  how good those guys are but that was one  of those unique moments and I kind of  look back on and laugh and cherish  because who gets to catch passes with  Tom Brady at Augusta National 7:15 in  the morning but imagine if he breaks  your finger and somebody says oh how did  you break your finger Tom Brady threw a  pass at me yeah so that would that would  that wouldn't be good if you looked at  either way but it was two weeks before  the master so I didn't want the Masters  because of that single toughest golf  course in the world is where that's a  that's a hard question to answer but I  would actually say Torrey Pines in San  Diego is one of the hardest for me  because when it's at sea level at  seventy six hundred yards the ball  doesn't go very far so from the tips  it's an it's the longest course I've  ever played and when you throw in some  US Open rough and firm greens like we we  get I think that's the hardest course  I've seen but a lot of people would  argue Oakmont in Pennsylvania and it  would be hard to disagree with that  I played Oakmont it makes you want to  cry like you think you're hitting a good  shot honey like you guys play it when  the greens are really fast when the  conditions are really difficult and I  played and I went I like it was a  slugfest like I was drained when I got  through I didn't enjoy it at all it's  beautiful I just didn't enjoy it so I  would agree with that is that hard  doesn't always mean better or fun I  think Oakmont maybe Torrey Pines  the hardest course it doesn't mean I  enjoy playing in fact I only like to  play once a year because it's not fun  and it beats you up every single shot  but I guess it's nice to have the  ability to be tested like that but I  always enjoy having a course that you  can reach some par fours or you you know  and and par fives you can get to and  make some birdies and short par threes  things like that those are always the  fun hold the match champions for charity  and be played May 2014 T and TBS the  medalists golf club in Florida Tigers  home course Peyton Manning Phil  Mickelson Tom Brady when do you think  your relationship changed with Tiger so  I've always appreciated and respected  what he has done for the game of golf  and what he's done for me because when  he increased the ratings  nobody benefited more than I did on the  course and off the course and and then  there was a moment in in 1991 when I won  my first PGA Tour event the entire purse  was a million dollars in the first place  cheque was a hundred eighty thousand and  as I was starting my career I thought  and wondered if there was ever be a  point where we would have a first-place  cheque of a million dollars because all  these other athletes were signing for  these big contracts and and I did not  think it would happen in my career and  Tiger comes along gets golf on the front  page and next thing you know sponsors  want in and we're playing for a  million-plus  first place check every single week now  and it's and it's been like that for  quite some time and I've always  attributed that to him and as well as  the increased opportunities off the golf  course that he has provided so I've  always had this respect for what he's  done for me and my family because  nobody's benefited more but I think our  relationship turned around 2016 when we  were working together for the Ryder Cup  he was an assistant captain and we ended  up spending a lot of time on the phone  getting ready talking about players  picks strategies alternate shot and and  course set up and all these things and  getting an idea to see how prepared he  was and meticulous with each little  thing I had a new respect for him but  when we work together for a common goal  which was to win the Ryder Cup it  brought us closer I think that was kind  of the turning  you've got a sneaky team this year in  the NFL so my sneaky team is the same  team I'm just a charger fan  unfortunately they're not the San Diego  Chargers or Los Angeles but I'm still a  charger fan I always think they're going  to be better than they are they lose  more games by a single score than any  team in the NFL and they lead in  injuries so if they can get that fixed  they have so much talent I think they  can be really fun I don't know much  about the quarterback that they drafted  Justin Herbert other than he's a really  really smart guy and a lot of talent and  I think that's a formula for success  he's got a lot of weapons around him and  I really like the GM Tom's Lesko I think  he's just a brilliant guy and he he he  brings in a lot of talent that adds to  the team not just first string but  second string to a lot of depth and I  just think they're a really good team  but they just don't play to their level  of talent or ability it just surprises  me so yet I'm here I am stuck pulling  for them every year and wish them well  if I said you could win another major or  the Chargers could win the Superbowl so  I'm a pretty selfish ass how about just  a regular tour stop or Chargers win the  Super Bowl  so if you had asked me you know years  ago about that I would say the Super  Bowl but because I'm 50 and I kind of  cherish each win from here on out I  would take a regular tour with it's  great to catch up with you I know it's  for the all in challenge and making a 10  million dollar donation to coronavirus  relief efforts and should be fun it's  great to talk to you again Phil I hope  families do well and thanks for your  time and thank for having me back on  your shows nice to be back on that's a  phil mickleson

